WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.368 --> 00:00:01.558
- Doin' what you love to do.

00:00:01.558 --> 00:00:03.015
Doin' what you love to do

00:00:03.015 --> 00:00:07.859
is the way that you're going
to give the best to the world!

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:09.793
And for me my brand is
about the live performance.

00:00:09.793 --> 00:00:10.758
It's about touring.

00:00:10.758 --> 00:00:13.185
It's about, you know, comin' out here

00:00:13.185 --> 00:00:16.250
and hostin' these parties and havin' fun

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.235
touchin' the people.

00:00:17.235 --> 00:00:20.113
Once you make this stuff,
put yourself out there

00:00:20.113 --> 00:00:21.990
so people can hear it!

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.550
- He's an American recording
artist and record producer

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.309
from Washington D.C.

00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:29.270
He's produced hit singles for
a number of prominent artists

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:30.830
in a variety of genres.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.571
He's also the founder
and CEO of SuperPhone,

00:00:33.571 --> 00:00:35.902
and NextSelection Lifestyle Group.

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.062
He's Ryan Leslie, and
here is my take on his

00:00:38.062 --> 00:00:39.661
Top 10 Rules For Success.

00:00:39.661 --> 00:00:41.318
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite!

00:00:41.318 --> 00:00:43.394
Also as Ryan is talking if
he's says something that's

00:00:43.394 --> 00:00:45.329
really meaningful to
you, please leave it in

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:47.226
the comments below, and
put quotes around it so

00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:49.815
other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:50.648
Enjoy!

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.350
(dramatic music)

00:01:03.861 --> 00:01:07.619
- At 15 years old, you went to Harvard,

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:10.369
scored 1600 on the SAT, graduated--

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:11.511
- Did your research!

00:01:11.511 --> 00:01:15.572
- Graduated at 19, and at 19 when you

00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:18.741
gave the commencement speech
at Harvard, you said that

00:01:18.741 --> 00:01:23.037
you wanted to be a performer,
and some people laughed.

00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:24.969
Tell me about that.

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:26.511
- Well I mean you just
have to think, you know,

00:01:26.511 --> 00:01:30.624
after four years and $120,000

00:01:30.624 --> 00:01:32.940
worth of, you know....

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.565
arguably one of the greatest
educations that the world

00:01:36.565 --> 00:01:38.911
has to offer, at Harvard.

00:01:38.911 --> 00:01:41.505
There's so many other opportunities

00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:44.902
and pathways that you
could take as a young,

00:01:44.902 --> 00:01:47.288
bright, and even an African American,

00:01:47.288 --> 00:01:50.118
you know, there's like, you
know, there's already so many

00:01:50.118 --> 00:01:53.176
African Americans that want
to rap, or that, you know,

00:01:53.176 --> 00:01:55.269
they want to make music.

00:01:55.269 --> 00:01:59.376
Why can't we have an incredible outlier?

00:01:59.376 --> 00:02:02.447
Why can't we have someone
who's, you know, an incredible

00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:03.422
spokesperson like other--

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:04.309
- But we can!

00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:07.009
- Another Barrack Obama
so to speak, you know?

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:09.622
And for me, it always
once again comes back to

00:02:09.622 --> 00:02:11.408
what we touched on before which is,

00:02:11.408 --> 00:02:12.922
doing what you love to do.

00:02:12.922 --> 00:02:14.822
Doing what you love to do is the way

00:02:14.822 --> 00:02:19.380
that you're going to give
the best to the world!

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.020
And I knew that anything else
that I did, whether it was

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.022
takin' a job or boss of
consulting group or, you know

00:02:26.022 --> 00:02:29.166
even the community service
and philanthropy initiatives

00:02:29.166 --> 00:02:30.680
in which I was involved.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.072
You know I have a
passion for those things,

00:02:32.072 --> 00:02:35.947
but none of them even remotely touches

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:40.058
the passion that I have for
live performance and music.

00:02:40.058 --> 00:02:43.008
Number two, hard work.

00:02:43.008 --> 00:02:44.127
'Cause guess what?

00:02:44.127 --> 00:02:48.294
The life that I live, goin'
from country to country,

00:02:49.393 --> 00:02:53.456
stage to stage, shootin'
music videos in the studio,

00:02:53.456 --> 00:02:55.790
it seemed like everywhere
I go people ask me,

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.202
"Ryan, how can I get that life?"

00:02:59.202 --> 00:03:01.988
The only way I was able to
get it was by working harder

00:03:01.988 --> 00:03:04.843
than everyone else who said
they wanted it, but didn't

00:03:04.843 --> 00:03:06.562
work hard enough to get it.

00:03:06.562 --> 00:03:11.110
My words of advice are really
the same words that I spoke on

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.533
when I was a teenager, and
I needed money for college.

00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:16.474
And so I head to the speech competition

00:03:16.474 --> 00:03:19.699
for Rotary International and...

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.345
the topic that they asked me

00:03:21.345 --> 00:03:22.928
to speak on, was...

00:03:24.424 --> 00:03:26.720
"Do what you believe in and
believe in what you do".

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.229
And even now 12 years
later, when I look around,

00:03:31.229 --> 00:03:33.803
that's what I strive to do
everyday, do what I believe in

00:03:33.803 --> 00:03:35.244
and believe in what I do.

00:03:35.244 --> 00:03:39.065
For everyone out there that has
an aspiration, do something!

00:03:39.065 --> 00:03:41.474
Don't let anyone tell you
what you can and cannot do!

00:03:41.474 --> 00:03:44.260
If you got a dream and a
goal and a passion to do it,

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.266
you know, I can't tell
you the amount of people

00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:47.274
that told me,

00:03:47.274 --> 00:03:51.059
"Look Ryan, stick to stand
behind the synthesizer or,

00:03:51.059 --> 00:03:53.821
if you go and sing, don't
try to rap, or if you rap,

00:03:53.821 --> 00:03:55.626
don't try to sing, or if you produce,

00:03:55.626 --> 00:03:59.868
don't try to be an artist,
if you're an R&amp;B dude,

00:03:59.868 --> 00:04:03.468
don't try to go into the
rock or alternative side,

00:04:03.468 --> 00:04:05.989
or if you don't dance, don't try to dance,

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:10.106
or if you dance, don't,
you know, don't decide it

00:04:10.106 --> 00:04:12.140
you just going to stay
behind the keyboard."

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.444
Really for me, I think
artistry, the beauty and the joy

00:04:15.444 --> 00:04:18.159
of artistry and the beauty
and the joy of just havin'

00:04:18.159 --> 00:04:21.735
the freedoms that we enjoy in
life, is that we can pursue

00:04:21.735 --> 00:04:24.786
anything that we want to pursue
and with the right mix of

00:04:24.786 --> 00:04:27.706
drive, desire, discipline, and dedication

00:04:27.706 --> 00:04:30.292
we can achieve anything that
we want to achieve and so for

00:04:30.292 --> 00:04:33.508
all you all out there that have
a dream, I encourage you to

00:04:33.508 --> 00:04:36.166
keep striving, pushing
forward toward that dream,

00:04:36.166 --> 00:04:37.813
and make it happen, man!

00:04:37.813 --> 00:04:40.725
There's a lot of rap artists,
or hip hop artist, you know,

00:04:40.725 --> 00:04:44.055
or urban music artists that go
overseas and they just assume

00:04:44.055 --> 00:04:47.807
that, you know, it's a
privilege for that market to

00:04:47.807 --> 00:04:51.717
have them there and they
don't really deliver on the,

00:04:51.717 --> 00:04:54.235
in terms of a live experience,
you know, they come over,

00:04:54.235 --> 00:04:56.754
they might give a half an
hour show, 40 minute show,

00:04:56.754 --> 00:04:58.853
they do it to track, they go out there,

00:04:58.853 --> 00:05:01.396
they collect the money 'cause
it's good money out there,

00:05:01.396 --> 00:05:03.560
and they come home and
because their touring base

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.905
is in the States, they
leave a lot of fans,

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.576
really disappointed, you know?

00:05:07.576 --> 00:05:11.743
And it's been my strategy to
go out there and really just

00:05:13.078 --> 00:05:16.154
shatter anyone else's
live performance, at least

00:05:16.154 --> 00:05:17.833
at the level that I'm doin' it right now,

00:05:17.833 --> 00:05:21.282
we doin' 1,200 to 2,000 seaters, you know,

00:05:21.282 --> 00:05:25.102
maybe 3,000 in some markets,
and really just shatter

00:05:25.102 --> 00:05:29.078
everyone's expectations
about what egotism, you know,

00:05:29.078 --> 00:05:32.158
an American artist might
have by comin' out there,

00:05:32.158 --> 00:05:35.755
and we've built an incredible
tourin' base, you know.

00:05:35.755 --> 00:05:39.067
Honestly if you average
it out, we doin' probably

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:43.080
$1.2 million a month in Europe, you know,

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.592
if we go out there for a month.

00:05:44.592 --> 00:05:48.409
So, that's 40,000 tickets
at 35 Euros a ticket

00:05:48.409 --> 00:05:50.670
top-line, you know, you do the math.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.009
- [Interviewer] I also read
that this new project you

00:05:53.009 --> 00:05:55.280
plan to release each song with a video?

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.313
- Absolutely.

00:05:56.313 --> 00:05:57.655
- [Interviewer] For free?

00:05:57.655 --> 00:05:58.488
- Absolutely.

00:05:58.488 --> 00:06:01.373
- [Interviewer] So why do
take this major bet like you

00:06:01.373 --> 00:06:02.493
giving music?

00:06:02.493 --> 00:06:05.472
- Well I mean, really, if
you look at the economics

00:06:05.472 --> 00:06:09.099
of the music business right
now, you're really making money

00:06:09.099 --> 00:06:11.538
when you're on the road,
and when you're touring.

00:06:11.538 --> 00:06:14.621
And so, it's like...

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.542
Have you ever paid to download
a Coca-Cola commercial?

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:20.375
No.

00:06:20.375 --> 00:06:25.005
Have you ever paid to download
a commercial for a car?

00:06:25.005 --> 00:06:25.838
No.

00:06:25.838 --> 00:06:28.038
So, all the money that they spend

00:06:28.038 --> 00:06:32.052
in creating an advertisement
for just a can of Coke

00:06:32.052 --> 00:06:33.868
or Pepsi, I mean, the Superbowl

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.685
just happened in the United States.

00:06:35.685 --> 00:06:37.408
All of the big advertisements,

00:06:37.408 --> 00:06:39.619
the amount of money that
they spend to create

00:06:39.619 --> 00:06:43.353
those advertisements, you're
watchin' them for free.

00:06:43.353 --> 00:06:48.165
And what it really is is a
call to action for you to

00:06:48.165 --> 00:06:51.473
buy into the brand, and for
me, my brand is about the

00:06:51.473 --> 00:06:54.146
live performance, it's
about touring, it's about,

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:57.818
you know, comin' out here
and hostin' these parties

00:06:57.818 --> 00:06:59.705
and havin' fun touchin' the people.

00:06:59.705 --> 00:07:03.196
And so, when people are
paying that, you just really

00:07:03.196 --> 00:07:07.066
have to think, when someone
pays $20 to come to the club,

00:07:07.066 --> 00:07:09.682
that's double the price of
an album, you know, and they

00:07:09.682 --> 00:07:13.693
might do that three of four
times a year, as opposed to

00:07:13.693 --> 00:07:15.947
buying your album one time for 10 dollars.

00:07:15.947 --> 00:07:18.797
So I'm lookin' at this
as an advertisement for,

00:07:18.797 --> 00:07:22.373
Ryan Leslie the brand, Ryan
Leslie just the personality

00:07:22.373 --> 00:07:24.696
'cause I like to come out, you know,

00:07:24.696 --> 00:07:27.363
(speaks French)

00:07:30.038 --> 00:07:33.571
I want to be able to touch the
people, and I want people to

00:07:33.571 --> 00:07:36.067
have a reason to want to
come and interact with me.

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:37.815
Yeah, so I look at it that way.

00:07:37.815 --> 00:07:40.021
- [Interviewer] Okay, so you
plan the future to no more

00:07:40.021 --> 00:07:42.521
rely on the album or CDs or...

00:07:43.399 --> 00:07:46.440
- I would say there's very
few artists that actually

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.942
even make money from record sales, right?

00:07:48.942 --> 00:07:52.495
Because the record labels
have given them an advance

00:07:52.495 --> 00:07:55.790
and they only make money
when they do shows,

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:58.149
or they do appearances, or
they sell t-shirts, you know?

00:07:58.149 --> 00:08:01.130
We go the "NS For Life"
hoodies, man, you look at my man

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.991
right there you get the "NS
For Life" hoodie, you know?

00:08:04.991 --> 00:08:07.523
It's something that you can't
download, that experience

00:08:07.523 --> 00:08:10.298
you can't download, and
I'm not stupid, man.

00:08:10.298 --> 00:08:12.628
I know what's goin' on, I
mean, you know, when you

00:08:12.628 --> 00:08:14.834
look at the streaming
services, when you look at the

00:08:14.834 --> 00:08:17.897
file sharing services, people
are going to share your stuff

00:08:17.897 --> 00:08:20.721
anyways digital, you know,
whether it's a photograph,

00:08:20.721 --> 00:08:23.888
or a song so the concert experience...

00:08:24.782 --> 00:08:27.404
that's something that you
can't download, you know?

00:08:27.404 --> 00:08:29.514
Either you have a ticket
to the Jay-Z concert,

00:08:29.514 --> 00:08:31.891
or you just at home, you know what I mean?

00:08:31.891 --> 00:08:33.545
You can't download that experience.

00:08:33.545 --> 00:08:37.741
So, my interest in making an
album with all the visuals

00:08:37.741 --> 00:08:41.591
and all the music, is to create a demand

00:08:41.591 --> 00:08:45.706
for that concert experience
that you can't download.

00:08:45.706 --> 00:08:47.842
- So you're not strictly an artist?

00:08:47.842 --> 00:08:49.207
You're a businessman!

00:08:49.207 --> 00:08:50.357
- Yeah!

00:08:50.357 --> 00:08:53.477
- What could you really say in terms of

00:08:53.477 --> 00:08:56.191
handling your business in an
economy like this that people

00:08:56.191 --> 00:08:58.281
really have to focus on these days?

00:08:58.281 --> 00:08:59.966
Cause I think very few
people who are watching

00:08:59.966 --> 00:09:01.764
this right now could really say, you know,

00:09:01.764 --> 00:09:04.385
I'm good for 10 years, financially.

00:09:04.385 --> 00:09:05.876
Most people are living
paycheck to paycheck,

00:09:05.876 --> 00:09:07.204
month to month, they may have a little bit

00:09:07.204 --> 00:09:09.202
of savings but they're not sitting on

00:09:09.202 --> 00:09:11.736
a decade's worth of living expenses.

00:09:11.736 --> 00:09:14.023
- You know it also depends on what you're

00:09:14.023 --> 00:09:16.644
living expenses are, you
know, I live within my means.

00:09:16.644 --> 00:09:17.488
You know what I mean? So--

00:09:17.488 --> 00:09:19.086
- So that's the first thing.

00:09:19.086 --> 00:09:21.199
The first thing is that you
got to live within your means.

00:09:21.199 --> 00:09:24.984
- Yeah, you know, so, if
you decide that, you know,

00:09:24.984 --> 00:09:28.811
and I have friends that
decide they want to be

00:09:28.811 --> 00:09:32.236
in the penthouse before
they can afford to really be

00:09:32.236 --> 00:09:34.659
in the penthouse but they can
afford to be in the penthouse

00:09:34.659 --> 00:09:37.462
for a year maybe, so they
go and do that for a year.

00:09:37.462 --> 00:09:41.254
And, I mean, to each their
own, everybody has the

00:09:41.254 --> 00:09:43.482
prerogative to live
however they want to live.

00:09:43.482 --> 00:09:47.436
So, we're not promised tomorrow,
so there are a lot of people

00:09:47.436 --> 00:09:50.131
who just have the idea,
"Hey, let me live for today!"

00:09:50.131 --> 00:09:50.964
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:09:50.964 --> 00:09:51.797
- Fault to a fault.

00:09:51.797 --> 00:09:52.853
- Yeah, you know what I'm sayin'?

00:09:52.853 --> 00:09:53.760
So, it's all good!

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.922
I mean, for me it's just about
just bein' responsible and

00:09:57.922 --> 00:10:01.257
cognizant of what you
need to be happy, yeah?

00:10:01.257 --> 00:10:04.519
And so, for me, all I need is,

00:10:04.519 --> 00:10:07.076
the ability to keep creating,

00:10:07.076 --> 00:10:08.745
and uh, I'm good.

00:10:08.745 --> 00:10:11.373
- The first time I met
you, you told me that,

00:10:11.373 --> 00:10:13.933
this is when you were a
producer for Bad Boy, and

00:10:13.933 --> 00:10:18.118
you told me that Tommy Mottola
and Puff used to always

00:10:18.118 --> 00:10:20.422
tell you, "Why do you
want to be an artist?

00:10:20.422 --> 00:10:22.386
You're hustling backwards!"

00:10:22.386 --> 00:10:25.969
What did they mean by that,
and why did you persist to

00:10:25.969 --> 00:10:27.803
continue to become an artist?

00:10:27.803 --> 00:10:31.274
- Well I think they really,
being the luminaries

00:10:31.274 --> 00:10:34.962
that they are, and the moguls
that they are, you know

00:10:34.962 --> 00:10:36.994
they were talking much
more in terms of just

00:10:36.994 --> 00:10:38.730
the generation of income,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.420
and for me, generating income

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.420
in a way that, you know,
is just left of center

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.006
of what you love to do

00:10:47.006 --> 00:10:49.321
is not the way I want to live, you know?

00:10:49.321 --> 00:10:52.799
And I feel like the American
dream, which is, you know,

00:10:52.799 --> 00:10:57.105
what I really consider
myself the embodiment of,

00:10:57.105 --> 00:10:59.794
I feel like the American
dream is bein' able to

00:10:59.794 --> 00:11:01.936
make a living doin' what you love to do.

00:11:01.936 --> 00:11:03.648
And so for me, production was

00:11:03.648 --> 00:11:07.416
always just a stepping
stone to amass enough money

00:11:07.416 --> 00:11:10.986
so that eventually as the
music industry shifted,

00:11:10.986 --> 00:11:14.243
I could buy my contract out,
and reinvest all the money that

00:11:14.243 --> 00:11:18.045
I had earned into doing what
I really love to do which is

00:11:18.045 --> 00:11:20.812
bein' on stage playin', and you know just

00:11:20.812 --> 00:11:22.404
goin' runnin' around the world

00:11:22.404 --> 00:11:25.283
and playin' shows for screamin' audiences

00:11:25.283 --> 00:11:26.717
all over the world.

00:11:26.717 --> 00:11:29.717
When you want to get your music out...

00:11:30.685 --> 00:11:35.555
people usually think it two
ways, radio and television.

00:11:35.555 --> 00:11:39.406
In 2010, there's a new way,
it's called the Internet.

00:11:39.406 --> 00:11:40.399
(assembly laughs)

00:11:40.399 --> 00:11:41.358
Right?

00:11:41.358 --> 00:11:42.799
Youtube!

00:11:42.799 --> 00:11:44.436
Right?

00:11:44.436 --> 00:11:48.603
So once you make your stuff,
once you make your stuff,

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:52.937
put yourself out there
so people can hear it!

00:11:52.937 --> 00:11:57.452
And if enough people respond,
I'll give you three examples,

00:11:57.452 --> 00:11:58.369
Soulja Boy.

00:11:59.807 --> 00:12:02.628
- [Audience Member] Justin Bieber!

00:12:02.628 --> 00:12:04.017
- Justin Bieber.

00:12:04.017 --> 00:12:06.850
(assembly laughs)

00:12:10.669 --> 00:12:12.396
And also,

